From 0ba3e932b1f7b612ed38e20e1e4f2e004adcf04e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:37:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 46/48] feat(audio): port Canary's XMP title-playlist player

Titles that ship their own soundtrack hand it to the XMP (Xbox Music
Player) as a title playlist and ask the system to play it: PGR4 registers
115 WMA tracks with XMPCreateTitlePlaylist and then drives them with
XMPPlayTitlePlaylist, XMPNext, XMPPause and XMPContinue. The ReXGlue XmpApp
stored the playlists and reported a playing state but decoded nothing
("Playlist playback not supported"), so those titles had no music.

This ports the player from Xenia Canary 451f915:

- src/xenia/apu/audio_media_player.{h,cc} -> rex/audio/audio_media_player
  (AudioMediaPlayer: playlists, play/pause/next/previous, repeat and
  shuffle, per-controller settings, state-change notifications).
- src/xenia/kernel/xam/apps/xmp_app.{h,cc} -> the XmpApp message layer
  (0x70002-0x70053, XMPAccessCheck, the XMP_* argument structures and the
  XMP notification IDs).

AudioSystem owns the player, creates it in Setup and shuts it down before
its own drivers go. Songs are opened through the guest file system, decoded
with libavformat/libavcodec (wmav2, wmapro, mp3) and fed to a host-format
driver from AudioSystem::CreateDriver at the song's own rate and channel
count; the pacing driver stands in when no device opens.

Where the port differs from Canary:

- The worker handshake is a mutex, a condition variable and a stop serial
  instead of Canary's busy fences, so a stopped or idle player sleeps.
- The demuxer reads the song through AVIO callbacks over one buffer, and
  decoded samples go through one reusable vector; nothing is allocated per
  frame. The decoder is drained at end of file and the last partial frame
  is zero-padded rather than dropped.
- The end of a song waits until the driver has returned every queued
  frame (with a stall timeout unless paused), so a track is not cut short
  before the next one starts.
- XMP_SONGINFO.handle is big-endian (Canary stores a native X_HANDLE), the
  song format is read once through rex::be (Canary swaps it twice), and
  XMPGetCurrentSong writes at most 39 characters per string so the
  terminator survives and a long tag cannot run into the next field.
- With audio disabled there is no player; XmpApp answers X_E_FAIL and
  warns once instead of dereferencing null.
- The XMPCaptureOutput callback stays disabled, as in Canary.

New cvars: enable_xmp (Canary's) and xmp_default_volume, which stands in
for the dashboard music volume Canary reads from XCONFIG (default 0.7)
until the title sets one.

xtypes.h gains X_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED, X_E_ACCESS_DENIED and
X_E_NOT_SUPPORTED from Canary's xbox.h for the message layer.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
